Short:        WinUAE HW 3D: Warp3D/MiniGL/GLES2/GLES3
Author:       Serkan DURSUN, based on QuarkTex by Robert Konrad
Uploader:     Serkan DURSUN <blasterreal@gmail.com>
Type:         driver/video
Version:      1.2beta1
Architecture: m68k-amigaos >= 3.0
Distribution: Aminet
Requires:     WinUAE with native code, Picasso96 RTG, 68020+ with FPU

QuarkTex NG 1.2 beta 1
======================

QuarkTex NG gives AmigaOS in WinUAE hardware accelerated 3D: Warp3D,
StormMESA (agl.library), MiniGL, OpenGL ES 2.0 (ogles2.library) and
OpenGL ES 3.0/3.1 (ogles3.library) programs draw with the PC's graphics
card through OpenGL, instead of the emulated CPU drawing every pixel.

It is an independent continuation of QuarkTex, the 3D virtualization
solution Robert Konrad released in 2003 (driver/video/QuarkTex, last
version 0.53), under the same license, the GNU LGPL v3.

Thanks to R-Typer and Ladislawv
-------------------------------

ogles2.library and ogles3.library implement the interfaces of the PiStorm
ogles2.library and ogles3.library and their SDKs, made by R-Typer and
Ladislawv for the PiStorm with a Raspberry Pi 4:

  ogles2  OpenGL ES 2.0  https://github.com/ladislawv/ogles2  (1.3) and
                         https://github.com/ladislawv/ogles2pi3 (1.4)
  ogles3  OpenGL ES 3.1  https://github.com/ladislawv/ogles3  (release 1.0)

QuarkTex NG's libraries are built against their SDKs' headers and tested
with their examples and demos, so programs written for their libraries
also run in WinUAE. Many thanks to both of them.

What is new in 1.2 beta 1
-------------------------

- Warp3D: BlitzQuake draws again. W3D_ClearZBuffer clears the z-buffer
  also while W3D_ZBUFFERUPDATE is off, as Warp3D does; BlitzQuake clears
  it in that state, so nothing was cleared and the picture stayed as
  stripes of one colour. Thanks to Lucas "Ponki" for the report.
- ogles2.library 1.4: the version of the PiStorm ogles2.library 1.4 by
  R-Typer and Ladislawv, built with its 1.4 beta 2 SDK (the interface is
  1.3's). Programs that bring a 1.4 library in their drawer, as Doom 3
  does, use the installed one.
- ogles2/ogles3: anisotropic filtering and S3TC textures (DXT1, DXT3,
  DXT5). Doom 3's timedemo: 55 fps against 45 fps without them.
- ogles2/ogles3: multisampling as the PiStorm 1.4 library has it, by the
  context tag OGLES2_CCT_PISTORM_MSAA or "SetEnv OGLES2/MSAA 4" (2 up to
  the graphics card's maximum).
- No false FPU warning for programs that set double precision themselves
  (Doom 3) with WinUAE's FPU set to "Host (80-bit)".
- This is a beta: the last normal release is 1.1.

What was new in 1.1
-------------------

- New ogles3.library: OpenGL ES 3.0 and 3.1, the interface of the PiStorm
  ogles3.library 1.0 by R-Typer and Ladislawv, on the PC's OpenGL 4.3.
  All 380 entries of its table: GLSL ES 3.00/3.10 shaders, vertex array
  objects, uniform buffers, instancing, transform feedback, queries,
  samplers, 3D and array textures, multiple render targets, and ES 3.1's
  compute shaders, storage buffers, atomic counters, images, indirect
  draws and dispatches, separate shader programs, multisample textures.
  The ogles3 1.0 demos (Boing 2026, Boing Ocean, Boing Swarm, Juggler RT)
  run unchanged, in a window and fullscreen. As the PiStorm library, a
  context is OpenGL ES 3.1 unless it asks for 2.0.
- ogles2.library:
  - Faster drawing from vertex buffers: the host keeps their converted
    copies and converts only what a draw reads and what changed. Quake III
    "timedemo four": 98 fps against 83 fps with 1.0 beta 3 on the same PC.
  - Every entry of the SDK is there now: ETC1 compressed textures (ETC2
    and EAC too in ogles3), GL_KHR_debug, glMultiDraw*EXT, base vertex
    draws, glClipControlEXT (with an OpenGL 4.5 driver).
  - OGLES2_CCT_SHARE_WITH: contexts share textures, buffers, shaders,
    programs and renderbuffers.
  - Contexts on a bitmap (OGLES2_CCT_BITMAP, aglSetBitmap) and resizing
    with aglSetParams2 work and are tested.
  - The dispatch table's calls go to the context they are given: a second
    task can draw with its own context.
  - Framebuffer 0 is the context's picture: programs that draw into a
    texture and then bind framebuffer 0 again show their frames.
- minigl.library 30.0: MiniGL's 30 SDK (PiStorm3D 30.0), same interface
  as the 29 SDK.
- agl.library: contexts on a rastport (AMA_RastPort, AMA_Screen), as
  StormMESA allowed.
- Windows in front of a 3D window stay as they are: frames are written
  only into the visible parts of a window.
- QuarkTexNGCheck.exe: tells whether the PC's graphics driver can run
  QuarkTex NG.
- The host uses OpenGL 4.3 (3.3 still runs everything but OpenGL ES 3.1).
- Libraries and DLLs of this release only work with each other: install
  all of them.

What is new since QuarkTex 0.53
-------------------------------

- Runs in 64-bit WinUAE as well (0.53 needed the 32-bit one).
- Commands go to the host in batches instead of one emulator call per
  OpenGL function; Warp3D is drawn on the host with a modern OpenGL.
- New minigl.library: MiniGL games such as Return to Castle Wolfenstein,
  Jedi Knight II, OpenLara and Hurrican run on the host's OpenGL, with
  multitexture, paletted textures and vertex arrays drawn by the host.
- New ogles2.library and ogles3.library (OpenGL ES 2.0, 3.0 and 3.1).
- Frames are written into the Amiga's RTG display memory, so the 3D picture
  shows in a window, a full window and fullscreen, scaled and filtered by
  WinUAE like any other Amiga graphics (0.53: windowed mode only).
- Several 3D programs can draw at the same time, each in its own window.
- Stencil buffer, chroma keying, mipmaps and several 3D contexts at once.
- Fixed: black fog, wrong texture colours, ignored palettes and texture
  updates, broken Z-buffer reads and writes, depth writes that could not be
  turned off, leaked textures, W3D_Query answers.

Performance (Intel Iris Xe, 68040/68060 with JIT)
-------------------------------------------------

  Warp3D, 1.2 million triangles  QuarkTex 0.53 1.34 s, QuarkTex NG 0.39 s
  RTCW at 640x480, no frame cap  MiniGL Classic on Warp3D 157 fps,
                                 QuarkTex NG minigl.library about 170 fps
  RTCW, data per frame to host   1.37 MB before, 22 KB now
  Quake III GLES2, timedemo four PiStorm with Raspberry Pi 4 80.4 fps,
  at 640x480                     QuarkTex NG 1.0 beta 3: 79.0 (window),
                                 83.6 (fullscreen)

Requirements
------------

- Windows with a graphics card and driver for OpenGL 4.3 (with 3.3 only,
  everything but OpenGL ES 3.1 runs; QuarkTexNGCheck.exe tells).
- WinUAE (tested with 6.0.3 and the 6.x betas), 32-bit or 64-bit.
- AmigaOS 3.x, a 68020 or better with FPU (68040/68060 recommended),
  Picasso96 with an RTG board (uaegfx).

Installation
------------

0. Run QuarkTexNGCheck.exe on the PC. It shows the graphics card, driver,
   OpenGL and GLSL versions and ends with "QuarkTex NG support = PASS" if
   the driver can run QuarkTex NG (OpenGL 4.3 core profile), or FAIL and
   why. Double-clicked it opens a window; in a command prompt it prints
   the report.
1. Copy the two files in WinUAE/ (quarktexng-windows-x86.dll and
   quarktexng-windows-x86-64.dll) into your WinUAE directory, next to
   winuae.exe and winuae64.exe (or into its plugins directory).
2. Copy the five files in Libs/ (Warp3D.library, agl.library,
   minigl.library, ogles2.library, ogles3.library) to LIBS: on your Amiga.
   Keep copies of the libraries you replace. The libraries and the DLLs
   belong together.
3. Set up WinUAE as below.

WinUAE settings
---------------

Required:
- Settings, Host, Miscellaneous: "Allow native code" (native_code=true).
- Settings, Hardware, CPU and FPU: FPU "CPU internal" with the precision
  "Host (80-bit)" (fpu_msvc_long_double=true). With the default 64-bit
  emulation games compute values a real 68k FPU does not: in Jedi Knight II
  the first person weapon never shows, in RTCW it disappears. The libraries
  check this and write a warning into QuarkTexNGLog.txt.

Recommended:
- CPU 68040 or 68060 at "Fastest possible".
- JIT on, cache size 16 MB (cachesize=16384), JIT "FPU support" on
  (compfpu=true). The games themselves run on the emulated 68k: with the
  JIT FPU off, Jedi Knight II fell from 90 to about 15 fps.
- RTG board UAE Zorro III, 64 MB or more (needed for fullscreen).
- Z3 Fast RAM, 256 MB or more for large games.

README.md in the archive explains every setting and has the full
performance tables.

Known issues
------------

- MiniGL Classic (the Warp3D based minigl.library) crashes in fullscreen on
  QuarkTex NG's Warp3D.library. Use the minigl.library of this archive.
- On planar or 8-bit screens the 3D picture is drawn in a window of its own
  over WinUAE, which shows only while WinUAE runs in a window.
- The rotating passport in OpenLara's menu draws dark.
- A smart refresh window's parts hidden behind other windows are not
  updated while they are covered.
- ogles2/ogles3.library: no shader or program binary formats;
  glClipControlEXT needs an OpenGL 4.5 driver; VSYNC and multisampling of
  the default framebuffer are ignored.
- Breaking Quake III (ogles2) with Ctrl-C during play reset the Amiga; a
  normal quit does not.

License and source
------------------

QuarkTex (C) 2003-2012 Robert Konrad.
QuarkTex NG, changes and additions since 2026 (C) Serkan DURSUN.

Both are released under the GNU Lesser General Public License version 3
(License.txt), which builds on the GNU General Public License version 3
(COPYING). QuarkTex NG is a modified version of QuarkTex and is not made or
endorsed by its original author.
ogles2.library and ogles3.library implement the interfaces of the PiStorm
ogles2.library and ogles3.library and their SDKs by R-Typer and Ladislawv
(github.com/ladislawv/ogles2, github.com/ladislawv/ogles3).
The notices of the parts made by others, among them the mingw-w64 runtime
in the host DLLs, are in ThirdParty.txt.

The source code of this release is in Source/ and on
https://github.com/Sdursun/QuarkTexNG. Left out of Source/ are Warp3D.h
and Warp3D.fd, the Warp3D API includes, (C) 1998 Sam Jordan, Hans-Joerg
Frieden and Thomas Frieden, which are not under the LGPL: building needs
them from QuarkTex's original repository, as
Source/Warp3D.library/Warp3D-includes.txt explains. Amigamesa.h is from
Mesa 1.2, (C) 1995 Brian Paul, LGPL v2 or later. Building minigl.library
needs MiniGL's SDK, (C) Hyperion under the Hyperion MiniGL Open Source
License, which is not included; building ogles2.library and ogles3.library
needs the ogles2 and ogles3 SDKs (MIT headers), not included either.

